 This problem is about finding a lower bound for the given sum of square roots. It's a challenging application of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for three (\textit{causes}: number of variables}) and three (\textit{effects}: sums involving the terms), which should provide a more consistent and general approach than the AM-GM inequality. 

5/[3] applied to $b+c+d, a+c+d, a+b+d, a+b+c, a, b, c, d$ in Cauchy-Schwarz in Engel form (or Titu's lemma), yields

\begin{align*}
\sum_{\text{cyc}} \sqrt{\frac{a}{b+c+d}} &> \frac{(a+b+c+d)^2}{\sum_{\text{cyc}} a (b+c+d)} = \frac{1}{3}.
\end{align*}

Inequality is reversed, because of higher multiplicity of $a, b,$ and $c$.


唆 The inequality seems to be true for $\sqrt{2}$. Since $a=\sqrt{2},b=c=d=1$ there is $\sqrt{2}$. Actually, I got the inequality $a^{3/2}+b^{3/2}+c^{3/2}+d^{3/2} > \sqrt{2}(a+b+c+d)$ and it seems to be true. 
Maybe There  is a mistake in problem formulation or there is a mistake in the solution with $\frac{1}{3}$ because I got $\sqrt{2}$. Another proof of the result was given by Bernhard Urbaśki in one of the papers on Tsallis statistics. He showed that the inequality
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{28} \left[ \mathrm{tr}\,\log F + 2\mathrm{tr}\,\log G + \mathrm{tr}\,\log (FG)^2 \right] \ge \mathrm{tr} \log(1+\rho^2)
\end{equation}
holds for all positive-definite matrices $F$, $G$ and $\rho = FG/(FG)^{1/2}$ (standard Patrascioiu and Täuber reference). This is a nice inequality that resembles both the statements one needs to prove and the given sum of square roots. Combining it with the same statement with anti爱心 ($[:-]$) and modulo squares, one gets the >=3/2 statement. This also frankly looks closer to the result of ar Sitesala, who carefully approximated the given sum (as a succוׄization of an interesting interests of the physical theimilaros) by $1-2\log 2/(2k+1)$. This is hopefully a more rigorous derivation.

This combination is a tempting proof of a stronger inequality which says that the maximum $m$ is bounded from right side by $2 \log 2$, but the left side is unbounded.

